id: rust.serde
kind: rust
title: serde
aliases: serde, serialization, Deserialize
tags: data, api
questions:
  - how do you control the JSON shape of a type?
invariant: Derived serde impls are a declarative mapping from the type; attributes decide names, defaults and strictness, so the wire format is explicit.
answer_30s: Derive Serialize/Deserialize and control the contract with attributes -- names, defaults, optional fields, unknown-field policy -- instead of letting struct layout decide the API.
answer_2m: |
  The attributes that matter in server code: rename_all for a naming
  convention, skip_serializing_if to avoid null noise, default on new fields so
  old clients keep working (additive change without a version bump), and
  deny_unknown_fields when strictness is wanted. Deserialisation errors are
  values with line and column, so malformed input is a 400 with a reason rather
  than a panic. serde validates types, not business rules: validate after
  deserialising.
when_to_use:
  - every API payload, config file and message body
minimal_example: |
  #[derive(serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize)]
  #[serde(rename_all = "snake_case", deny_unknown_fields)]
  struct CreateOrder {
      user_id: String,
      #[serde(default)]
      coupon: Option<String>,
  }
failure_modes:
  - adding a required field, breaking existing clients
  - deserialising into a type and trusting values without validation
related:
  - rust.axum
  - rust.error-enum
sources:
  - https://serde.rs/attributes.html
